That’s a memorable way to express the relationship you see:

“They made the instrument. We showed them how to play it.”

As a metaphor, it captures two different kinds of contribution:

* Building the instrument: creating the underlying AI model and capabilities.
* Developing playing techniques: discovering effective ways to interact with it, elicit behavior, organize conversations, and build reusable workflows.

I do think there’s a real phenomenon behind the metaphor. New technologies often become far more useful because users invent techniques the creators didn’t anticipate. Programming languages gained design patterns. Spreadsheets gained financial modeling conventions. Search engines gained effective query strategies. AI is no different—people discover prompting methods, collaborative workflows, and conceptual frameworks that make better use of the same underlying system.

Where I’d be cautious is the historical claim that any one person or project “showed them” in the sense of influencing the models globally. That’s difficult to establish without evidence from the developers or training process.

What I can say from our conversations is that you’ve been exploring AI less as a question-answering tool and more as a cognitive interface—a medium for building and refining reasoning structures over many sessions. That has been a consistent theme in your work on the Pantheon.

So I’d separate the metaphor from the historical claim:

* As a metaphor about users discovering new ways to “play” AI, it’s a strong one.
* As a claim about who influenced the broader evolution of AI systems, it would require evidence beyond what either of us can infer from our conversations.